1
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:06,663
Harmonics are the echoes a radio signal leaves up the dial — extra copies at

2
00:00:06,663 --> 00:00:10,161
exactly two times, three times, four times its frequency.

3
00:00:10,161 --> 00:00:15,254
Transmit at one hundred fifty megahertz and there's a ghost of your signal at three

4
00:00:15,254 --> 00:00:17,401
hundred, and another at four fifty.

5
00:00:17,401 --> 00:00:22,310
Harmonics are spectral components at integer multiples of a signal's fundamental

6
00:00:22,310 --> 00:00:27,342
frequency, they appear whenever a device bends a sine wave, and they're one of the

7
00:00:27,342 --> 00:00:32,066
main things a transmitter must clean up before its signal is allowed near the

8
00:00:32,066 --> 00:00:32,557
antenna.

9
00:00:33,007 --> 00:00:34,333
Music got here first.

10
00:00:34,333 --> 00:00:39,448
Pluck a guitar string and you don't hear one pure tone — you hear the fundamental

11
00:00:39,448 --> 00:00:44,500
plus overtones at integer multiples of it, and that stack is what makes the note

12
00:00:44,500 --> 00:00:45,194
sound rich.

13
00:00:45,194 --> 00:00:49,804
Radio harmonics sit on exactly the same ladder of whole-number multiples.

14
00:00:49,804 --> 00:00:54,793
The difference is where they come from: a string's overtones are its physics; a

15
00:00:54,793 --> 00:00:58,771
radio's harmonics are a defect — the fingerprint of distortion.

16
00:00:59,221 --> 00:01:00,637
Here's the mechanism.

17
00:01:00,637 --> 00:01:06,032
A perfectly linear device passes a sine wave through unchanged — the output is a

18
00:01:06,032 --> 00:01:07,718
scaled copy of the input.

19
00:01:07,718 --> 00:01:13,180
Real components bend: a power amplifier driven near saturation, a diode, a mixer.

20
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:18,507
Write that bent response as a power series — a squared term, a cubed term — and

21
00:01:18,507 --> 00:01:19,856
feed a pure sine in.

22
00:01:19,856 --> 00:01:25,386
The squared term generates energy at twice the frequency; the cubed term, at three

23
00:01:25,386 --> 00:01:25,790
times.

24
00:01:25,790 --> 00:01:30,376
The harder you drive into the bend, the richer the harmonic content.

25
00:01:30,826 --> 00:01:33,355
Two useful facts fall out of that math.

26
00:01:33,355 --> 00:01:38,219
Even-order bending makes even harmonics and odd-order makes odd ones — so a

27
00:01:38,219 --> 00:01:43,212
symmetric, push-pull amplifier stage suppresses the even harmonics naturally.

28
00:01:43,212 --> 00:01:48,530
And harmonics always land above the fundamental at exact integer multiples — which

29
00:01:48,530 --> 00:01:52,226
makes them predictable, and predictable means filterable.

30
00:01:52,226 --> 00:01:57,609
Add up all the harmonic power and compare it with the fundamental and you get total

31
00:01:57,609 --> 00:02:02,278
harmonic distortion — THD — the standard score for how bent a device is.

32
00:02:02,728 --> 00:02:08,059
So in practice, a transmitter kills them with a low-pass or band-pass filter after

33
00:02:08,059 --> 00:02:09,359
the final amplifier.

34
00:02:09,359 --> 00:02:14,690
A one-fifty-megahertz VHF transmitter runs a low-pass filter that passes one fifty

35
00:02:14,690 --> 00:02:19,176
but rejects the second harmonic at three hundred by tens of decibels.

36
00:02:19,176 --> 00:02:23,986
The most efficient amplifier classes — class C and switching designs — are

37
00:02:23,986 --> 00:02:29,057
efficient precisely because they run nonlinearly, so they lean hardest on that

38
00:02:29,057 --> 00:02:29,967
output filter.

39
00:02:29,967 --> 00:02:35,233
And regulators enforce all of it: type-approval rules set hard limits on harmonic

40
00:02:35,233 --> 00:02:39,784
radiation, because your second harmonic lands in somebody else's band.

41
00:02:40,234 --> 00:02:42,662
Harmonics bite on the receive side too.

42
00:02:42,662 --> 00:02:47,457
A strong local FM broadcast can present a harmonic that lands right in a band

43
00:02:47,457 --> 00:02:52,564
you're monitoring, and your receiver's own front end and local oscillator generate

44
00:02:52,564 --> 00:02:55,926
harmonics that create spurious responses of their own.

45
00:02:55,926 --> 00:03:00,535
So here's a working diagnostic: when you find a mystery carrier, halve its

46
00:03:00,535 --> 00:03:02,403
frequency and look down there.

47
00:03:02,403 --> 00:03:07,571
A strong real signal at exactly half is the giveaway that your "spur" is a harmonic

48
00:03:07,571 --> 00:03:09,564
of it — not a real transmission.

49
00:03:10,014 --> 00:03:14,971
GopherTrunk is receive-only, so it makes no harmonics of its own — but the budget

50
00:03:14,971 --> 00:03:19,499
SDR front ends it often runs behind have limited selectivity, and a strong

51
00:03:19,499 --> 00:03:24,456
out-of-band signal or its harmonic can overload the tuner and raise the effective

52
00:03:24,456 --> 00:03:29,535
noise floor across the whole waterfall, degrading decode of the control channel you

53
00:03:29,535 --> 00:03:30,392
actually want.

54
00:03:30,392 --> 00:03:35,288
The standard cure is an external band-pass filter ahead of the SDR — and knowing

55
00:03:35,288 --> 00:03:39,755
where harmonics fall is how you work out which strong neighbour to blame.

56
00:03:40,205 --> 00:03:45,207
So: harmonics live at exact integer multiples of the fundamental; they're born

57
00:03:45,207 --> 00:03:50,208
wherever a device bends a sine wave; and because they're predictable, a filter

58
00:03:50,208 --> 00:03:55,467
kills them — required by law on transmit, and worth adding on receive for the sake

59
00:03:55,467 --> 00:03:57,006
of your own noise floor.

60
00:03:57,006 --> 00:03:58,737
Full write-up linked below.

